{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 22, :comments ["<b>And Aharon lifted up his hands toward the people and blessed them</b> — the priestly blessing: יְבָרֶכְךָ / יָאֵר / יִשָּׂא. This is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Leviticus 9:22). If so, the passage \"Speak to Aharon and to his sons, saying: Thus shall you bless the children of Israel\" in the book of Numbers (Numbers 6:23) is chronologically earlier than this. And perhaps that is indeed so, for it is adjacent to what is said there (Numbers 7:1), \"And it came to pass on the day that Moshe finished setting up the Mishkan.\" And it is possible to say that Aharon spread his hands toward heaven and blessed the people, as Shelomo did, as it is said (I Kings 8:22), \"And Shelomo stood before the altar of YHWH and spread his hands toward heaven\" — and there (I Kings 8:55) it says, \"And he stood and blessed all the congregation of Israel with a loud voice, saying\" — and therefore Scripture did not mention that Moshe commanded him to do so. And in the Baraita of the passage of the ordination in Sifrei Kehunat Levi I found: \"'And he blessed them' — this is an unspecified blessing, which you do not know; Scripture turned back and specified it further on: 'May YHWH bless you and keep you / May YHWH make His face shine upon you / May YHWH lift up His face toward you,' etc.\" Yet I still have reason to say that they said this: this blessing with which Aharon blessed the people on his own initiative is unspecified, and Scripture did not explain to us what it was; but the blessing that the priests were commanded for all generations was specified, and it is identical for all priests forever. Or they maintain that here he was commanded regarding the lifting of hands for this day, and further on this blessing was given to him and to his sons for all generations."], :book :leviticus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 9, :notes {0 "The citation ויקרא ט':נ\"ה in the Hebrew is an error for מלכים א ח:נ\"ה (I Kings 8:55), which Ramban is clearly referencing; translated the verse citation as the author intended. The work referenced as ת\"כ is identified by the acronym as ספרא דבי רב or more likely Tanna d'Bei Kehunah (Sifrei Kehunat Levi); rendered as 'Sifrei Kehunat Levi' based on the parenthetical gloss."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:52:11.053988459Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}